(* tuvm#94 stage 2 — the bell strikes on the half-hour, from a local timer.

   No network. The claim is exactly one thing: THE BELL ALWAYS RINGS. Accuracy is
   stage 4's problem, and the architecture is built so that a WiFi outage costs
   accuracy and never reliability — the local timer is authoritative for striking,
   and NTP will only correct BETWEEN strikes.

   THE CLOCK IS A COUNTER, NOT A TIMESTAMP. `halfHours` counts half-hours from
   midnight, 0..47, and that is the whole of the node's notion of time. Deriving
   time-of-day from `monotonicMillis` instead would have been the obvious move and
   is worse twice over: it is an SMI on this target, so it wraps after ~12.4 days
   on a device meant to run for months, and it would make every strike depend on
   arithmetic that has to stay right across that wrap. A counter incremented once
   per tick has neither problem, and stage 4's correction becomes a single
   assignment to it rather than an epoch rebase.

   NO BOOT STRIKE, deliberately — unlike stage 1's gate node. The claim here is
   that the TIMER rings the bell, and a board that rings at start-up would sound
   like a working clock during the entire first interval, before the claim has
   been tested at all. `lastBells` is seeded to 8 so BUTTON_A still gives an
   immediate liveness check by hand.

   The 30-minute cadence is real (`halfHourMillis`), which makes the timer path
   unwitnessable in a sitting — so BUTTON_B advances the clock by one half-hour
   and strikes, through THE SAME method the timer calls. Eight presses walk the
   whole watch (1..8) in under a minute and prove the mapping and the phrasing;
   the timer path then proves its cadence over hours in the console log. A
   bench affordance, and marked as one: it is the only thing here that would not
   belong in a shipped appliance.

   WHY `every:` AND NOT A RESCHEDULED `after:`: the Zephyr backend arms a PERIODIC
   k_timer (`k_timer_start(&t, K_MSEC(ms), K_MSEC(ms))`, timer_zephyr.c:66), which
   re-arms from the previous EXPIRY, not from handler completion. A striking blocks
   ~2.5-4 s; with a periodic timer that eats into the interval and cannot shift the
   schedule, so there is no drift to correct for and no need to compute the next
   boundary by hand. Residual drift is the crystal's alone — 10-50 ppm on the
   40 MHz part, i.e. 0.02-0.09 s per half-hour.

   This node never returns, so it drains forever and cannot leak a live repeater
   the way TimerNode could. *)
class MagTagClockNode packageUsing: manifest = ( | halfHours ::= 0. lastBells ::= 8. | ) (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space bell timers gpio tick btnA btnB |
    space:: platform space.
    bell:: platform bell.
    timers:: platform timers.   (* hold ONE instance — it owns the pending registry *)
    gpio:: platform gpio.

    space whenever: 'sys/timer' do: [ :e | timers dispatch: e ].

    tick:: space channel: 'clock/tick'.
    tick whenever: '' do: [ :m | self advanceAndStrike: bell ].
    timers every: self halfHourMillis post: #tick on: tick.

    (* BUTTON_A repeats the last striking — stage 1's gate, kept as a regression
       and as the liveness check that covers the silent first interval. *)
    btnA:: space channel: 'btn/a'.
    btnA whenever: '' do: [ :m |
      ('repeat ' , lastBells printString) out.
      bell strike: lastBells ].
    gpio pin: 15 edge: #falling post: 'A' on: btnA.

    (* BUTTON_B fast-forwards one half-hour. Bench affordance — see the header. *)
    btnB:: space channel: 'btn/b'.
    btnB whenever: '' do: [ :m | self advanceAndStrike: bell ].
    gpio pin: 14 edge: #falling post: 'B' on: btnB.

    ('clock ready, ' , self halfHourMillis printString , ' ms per half-hour') out.
    (* `platform pumpEvents` pumps the backends INTO the space queue; `space drain`
       is what dequeues and fires reactions. A reactor loop without the drain
       enqueues ticks and presses forever and runs none of them. *)
    [ true ] whileTrue: [ platform pumpEvents. space drain ].
  )

  (* THE ONE PATH BOTH THE TIMER AND BUTTON_B TAKE. Keeping them on one method is
     what makes the button a witness for the timer rather than a second
     implementation that could agree with it by accident. *)
  advanceAndStrike: bell = (
    halfHours:: halfHours + 1.
    halfHours = 48 ifTrue: [ halfHours:: 0 ].
    lastBells:: bell bellsForHalfHour: halfHours.
    ((self timeString: halfHours) , ' strike ' , lastBells printString) out.
    bell strike: lastBells.
    'struck' out.
    ^ self )

  timeString: i = (
    | h |
    h:: i // 2.
    ^ (self pad: h) , (((i - (h * 2)) = 0) ifTrue: [ ':00' ] ifFalse: [ ':30' ]) )

  pad: n = ( (n < 10) ifTrue: [ ^ '0' , n printString ]. ^ n printString )

  (* REAL cadence. 1800000 ms = 30 minutes. *)
  halfHourMillis = ( ^ 1800000 )
) : (
)
